WEBVTT

00:00:00.180 --> 00:00:05.555
Put the camera at the origin, looking
along Z, with the image plane at distance
f.

00:00:05.555 --> 00:00:09.062
A ray from a 3D point to the centre
crosses that plane,

00:00:09.063 --> 00:00:11.231
and the two triangles are similar.

00:00:11.230 --> 00:00:13.868
So x equals f times X over Z.

00:00:13.868 --> 00:00:15.293
With f of 800 pixels,

00:00:15.294 --> 00:00:21.131
a point half a metre off-axis at four
metres lands one hundred pixels from the
centre.

00:00:21.089 --> 00:00:23.414
Now slide the point along its ray.

00:00:23.414 --> 00:00:27.707
The point one, zero, eight is twice as
wide and twice as far,

00:00:27.708 --> 00:00:29.890
and it lands on the same pixel.

00:00:29.889 --> 00:00:32.843
One image cannot separate size from
distance,

00:00:32.844 --> 00:00:37.965
which is why depth from one camera needs
learned priors or known object sizes.

00:00:37.922 --> 00:00:40.623
Pixel coordinates start at the image
corner,

00:00:40.624 --> 00:00:45.598
so the intrinsic matrix K adds the
principal point, here three twenty, two
forty.

00:00:45.597 --> 00:00:50.334
The camera point zero point five, minus
zero point two five, four,

00:00:50.335 --> 00:00:53.135
lands on pixel four twenty, one ninety.

00:00:53.134 --> 00:00:59.597
A six hundred and forty pixel wide image
then spans a horizontal view of about
forty-three point six degrees.

00:00:59.576 --> 00:01:01.674
A training pipeline crops the image,

00:01:01.675 --> 00:01:05.464
starting one hundred pixels from the left
and forty from the top.

00:01:05.463 --> 00:01:07.638
Where is the principal point in the crop?

00:01:07.638 --> 00:01:09.376
Pause and work it out.

00:01:10.896 --> 00:01:12.208
Subtract the offset:

00:01:12.208 --> 00:01:13.983
two twenty, two hundred.

00:01:13.983 --> 00:01:15.827
A crop or a resize changes K,

00:01:15.828 --> 00:01:20.534
so a network trained on crops with the old
K learns inconsistent geometry.

00:01:20.533 --> 00:01:25.271
Add a rotation and translation and you
have the full three by four camera matrix.

00:01:25.271 --> 00:01:28.822
With the camera five metres back, the
world point one, zero,

00:01:28.823 --> 00:01:31.959
zero gives twenty-four hundred, twelve
hundred, five.

00:01:31.958 --> 00:01:33.296
Divide by five:

00:01:33.296 --> 00:01:35.446
pixel four eighty, two forty.

00:01:35.340 --> 00:01:39.553
A rotation matrix has nine entries but
only three degrees of freedom.

00:01:39.553 --> 00:01:41.753
Here's a ninety degree turn about Z:

00:01:41.753 --> 00:01:45.053
its trace is one, so the cosine of theta
is zero.

00:01:45.053 --> 00:01:48.828
Axis-angle stores it in three numbers,
which suits optimizers.

00:01:48.828 --> 00:01:50.468
Euler angles are easy to read,

00:01:50.469 --> 00:01:53.804
but at ninety degrees of pitch they lose a
degree of freedom:

00:01:53.803 --> 00:01:55.003
gimbal lock.

00:01:55.003 --> 00:01:58.475
Unit quaternions use four numbers with no
gimbal lock,

00:01:58.476 --> 00:02:01.241
though q and minus q are the same
rotation.

00:02:01.196 --> 00:02:03.659
Real lenses bend straight lines.

00:02:03.659 --> 00:02:07.896
The radial model scales each point by its
distance from the centre.

00:02:07.896 --> 00:02:12.859
A negative coefficient pulls the edges in,
and the grid bows into a barrel.

00:02:12.859 --> 00:02:16.717
Calibration estimates these numbers from
photos of a checkerboard,

00:02:16.718 --> 00:02:18.472
and then we can undo the bend.
